{:edb/db-before-t 1088 :edb/db-after-t 1089 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534401 50 #inst "2026-10-07T00:28:06.363Z" 13194139534401 true] [17592186157109 1100 "Solve the inequality\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{5x^4 +2} \\lt \\dfrac{5-2x}{5x^4+2}.\n$$" 13194139534401 true] [17592186157109 1100 "Solve the inequality\n$\\dfrac{2}{5x^4 +2} \\lt \\dfrac{5-2x}{5x^4+2}$." 13194139534401 false] [17592186157109 1145 "We know that the denominator $5x^4+ 2$ is positive for all values of $x$. So, we can multiply both sides of the inequality by $5x^4+2$ and solve the resulting inequality as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{5x^{4} + 2} & < \\frac{5 - 2x}{5x^{4} + 2} \\\\ (5x^{4} + 2) \\cdot \\frac{2}{5x^{4} + 2} & < (5x^{4} + 2) \\cdot \\frac{5 - 2x}{5x^{4} + 2} \\\\ 2 & < 5 - 2x \\\\ 2x & < 3 \\\\ x & < \\frac{3}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139534401 false] [17592186157109 1145 "We know that the denominator $5x^4+ 2$ is positive for all values of $x.$ So, we can multiply both sides of the inequality by $5x^4+2$ and solve the resulting inequality as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{5{x}^{4}+2} & <\\frac{5-2x}{5{x}^{4}+2} \\\\ (5{x}^{4}+2)\\cdot \\frac{2}{5{x}^{4}+2} & <(5{x}^{4}+2)\\cdot \\frac{5-2x}{5{x}^{4}+2} \\\\ 2 & <5-2x \\\\ 2x & <3 \\\\ x & <\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534401 true] [17592186157110 1100 "Solve the inequality\n\n$$\n6x^3 + 3x \\lt 0.\n$$" 13194139534401 true] [17592186157110 1100 "Solve the inequality\n$6x^3 + 3x \\lt 0$." 13194139534401 false] [17592186157110 1145 "First, we factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{x}^{3}+3x & <0. \\\\ 3x(2{x}^{2}+1) & <0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $2x^2 + 1$ is always positive, we can divide both sides of the inequality by this quantity. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3x(2{x}^{2}+1)}{2{x}^{2}+1} & <\\frac{0}{2{x}^{2}+1} \\\\ 3x & <0 \\\\ x & <0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534401 true] [17592186157110 1145 "First, we factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}6x^{3} + 3x & < 0. \\\\ 3x(2x^{2} + 1) & < 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSince $2x^2 + 1$ is always positive, we can divide both sides of the inequality by this quantity. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}(3x(2x^{2} + 1))/(2x^{2} + 1) & < \\frac{0}{2x^{2} + 1} \\\\ 3x & < 0 \\\\ x & < 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139534401 false] [17592186157111 1100 "Solve the inequality\n\n$$\n(2\\sin{x}-9)(2x) \\leq 0.\n$$" 13194139534401 true] [17592186157111 1100 "Solve the inequality\n$(2\\sin{x}-9)(2x) \\leq 0$." 13194139534401 false] [17592186157111 1145 "Note that the factor $2\\sin{x}-9$ is always negative. We know this because\n\n$$\n-1 \\leq \\sin x \\leq 1 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad -11 \\leq 2\\sin x - 9 \\leq -7\n$$\n\nBecause $2\\sin{x}-9$ is always negative, we can divide both sides of the inequality by this quantity, but we have to remember to flip the sign of the inequality. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}(2\\sin x - 9)(2x) & \\le 0 \\\\ ((2\\sin x - 9)(2x))/(2\\sin x - 9) & \\ge \\frac{0}{2\\sin x - 9} \\\\ 2x & \\ge 0 \\\\ x & \\ge 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139534401 false] [17592186157111 1145 "Note that the factor $2\\sin{x}-9$ is always negative. We know this because\n\n$$\n-1 \\leq \\sin x \\leq 1 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad -11 \\leq 2\\sin x - 9 \\leq -7.\n$$\n\nBecause $2\\sin{x}-9$ is always negative, we can divide both sides of the inequality by this quantity, but we have to remember to flip the sign of the inequality. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-9)(2x) & \\le 0 \\\\ \\frac{(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-9)(2x)}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-9} & \\ge \\frac{0}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-9} \\\\ 2x & \\ge 0 \\\\ x & \\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534401 true] [17592186157112 1100 "Solve the inequality\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x+5}{1-3x} \\lt 2, \\quad x \\lt 0.\n$$" 13194139534401 true] [17592186157112 1100 "Solve the inequality\n\n$\\dfrac{x+5}{1-3x} \\lt 2, \\quad x \\lt 0$." 13194139534401 false] [17592186157112 1145 "Since $x\\lt 0$, we have that\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x & < 0 \\\\ -x & > 0 \\\\ -3x & > 0 \\\\ 1 - 3x & > 1\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nThis means that $1-3x$ is positive for all the values of $x$ that we are considering $(x\\lt 0)$. So, we can multiply both sides by $1-3x$, and solve the resulting inequality as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{x + 5}{1 - 3x} & < 2 \\\\ (1 - 3x) \\cdot \\frac{x + 5}{1 - 3x} & < (1 - 3x) \\cdot 2 \\\\ x + 5 & < 2 - 6x \\\\ 7x & < - 3 \\\\ x & < - \\frac{3}{7}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the inequality will be the numbers that satisfy both $x \\lt 0$ and\n\n$$\nx \\lt -\\dfrac{3}{7}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution to our inequality is\n\n$$\nx \\lt -\\dfrac{3}{7}\n$$" 13194139534401 false] [17592186157112 1145 "Since $x\\lt 0,$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & <0 \\\\ -x & >0 \\\\ -3x & >0 \\\\ 1-3x & >1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $1-3x$ is positive for all the values of $x$ that we are considering $(x\\lt 0).$ So, we can multiply both sides by $1-3x,$ and solve the resulting inequality as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+5}{1-3x} & <2 \\\\ (1-3x)\\cdot \\frac{x+5}{1-3x} & <(1-3x)\\cdot 2 \\\\ x+5 & <2-6x \\\\ 7x & <-3 \\\\ x & <-\\frac{3}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the inequality will be the numbers that satisfy both $x \\lt 0$ and $x \\lt -\\dfrac{3}{7}.$ So, the solution to our inequality is $x \\lt -\\dfrac{3}{7}.$" 13194139534401 true] [17592186157293 1020 #edb/ref 17592186286420 13194139534401 true] [17592186157293 1020 #edb/ref 17592186286425 13194139534401 true] [17592186157293 1020 #edb/ref 17592186286430 13194139534401 true] [17592186157293 1020 #edb/ref 17592186286436 13194139534401 true] [17592186157293 1020 #edb/ref 17592186286474 13194139534401 true] [17592186157294 1020 #edb/ref 17592186286441 13194139534401 true] [17592186157294 1020 #edb/ref 17592186286447 13194139534401 true] [17592186157294 1020 #edb/ref 17592186286460 13194139534401 true] [17592186157294 1020 #edb/ref 17592186286481 13194139534401 true] [17592186157295 1020 #edb/ref 17592186286392 13194139534401 true] [17592186157295 1020 #edb/ref 17592186286399 13194139534401 true] [17592186157295 1020 #edb/ref 17592186286406 13194139534401 true] [17592186157295 1020 #edb/ref 17592186286488 13194139534401 true] [17592186157295 1020 #edb/ref 17592186286495 13194139534401 true] [17592186157296 1020 #edb/ref 17592186286413 13194139534401 true] [17592186157296 1020 #edb/ref 17592186286452 13194139534401 true] [17592186157296 1020 #edb/ref 17592186286467 13194139534401 true] [17592186157914 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534401 true] [17592186157914 1100 "Solve the inequality\n\n$$\n\\frac{3x+2}{1-x}>4,\\,x>2.\n$$" 13194139534401 true] [17592186157914 1100 "Solve the inequality\n\n$\\frac{3x + 2}{1 - x} > 4, x > 2$." 13194139534401 false] [17592186157914 1145 "Since $x>2,$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & >2 \\\\ -x & <-2 \\\\ 1-x & <-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $1-x$ is negative for all the values of $x$ that we are considering $(x>2).$ So, we can multiply both sides by $1-x,$ provided that we flip the sign of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3x+2}{1-x} & >4 \\\\ (1-x)\\cdot \\frac{3x+2}{1-x} & <(1-x)\\cdot 4 \\\\ 3x+2 & <4-4x \\\\ 7x & <2 \\\\ x & <\\frac{2}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, there are no numbers that satisfy both $x>2$ and $x<\\frac{2}{7}.$ So, the inequality has no solution." 13194139534401 true] [17592186157922 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534401 true] [17592186157922 1100 "Solve the inequality\n\n$$\n\\frac{2x-3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)}\\ge 0,\\,x>3.\n$$" 13194139534401 true] [17592186157922 1100 "Solve the inequality\n\n$(2x - 3)/(\\ln (x - 2)) \\ge 0, x > 3$." 13194139534401 false] [17592186157922 1145 "Since $x>3,$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & >3 \\\\ x-2 & >3-2 \\\\ x-2 & >1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)$ is positive for all the values of $x$ that we are considering $(x>3).$ So, we can multiply both sides by $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2),$ and solve the resulting inequality as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x-3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)} & \\ge 0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)\\cdot \\frac{2x-3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)} & \\ge \\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)\\cdot 0 \\\\ 2x-3 & \\ge 0 \\\\ 2x & \\ge 3 \\\\ x & \\ge \\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the inequality will be the numbers that satisfy both $x>3$ and $x>\\frac{3}{2}.$ So, the solution to our inequality is $x>3.$" 13194139534401 true] [17592186157923 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534401 true] [17592186157923 1100 "Solve the inequality\n\n$$\n4x{e}^{2x}-5{e}^{2x}\\le 0.\n$$" 13194139534401 true] [17592186157923 1100 "Solve the inequality\n\n$4xe^{2x} - 5e^{2x} \\le 0$." 13194139534401 false] [17592186157923 1145 "First, we factor the given inequality, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x{e}^{2x}-5{e}^{2x} & \\le 0 \\\\ {e}^{2x}(4x-5) & \\le 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{2x}$ is always positive, we can divide both sides of the inequality by this quantity. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{e}^{2x}(4x-5)}{{e}^{2x}} & \\le \\frac{0}{{e}^{2x}} \\\\ 4x-5 & \\le 0 \\\\ 4x & \\le 5 \\\\ x & \\le \\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534401 true] [17592186286392 1017 #edb/ref 17592186286393 13194139534401 true] [17592186286392 1089 "q-116263" 13194139534401 true] [17592186286392 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534401 true] [17592186286392 1100 "Solve the inequality\n\n$$\n(-1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(|x|+2))(4x+7)\\le 0.\n$$" 13194139534401 true] [17592186286392 1113 #uuid "5b5529ad-b1a2-552f-b12b-dc7e0d4b23e0" 13194139534401 true] [17592186286392 1145 "Since $-1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(|x|+2)$ is always negative, we can divide both sides of the inequality by this quantity, but we have to remember to flip the sign of the inequality. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(|x|+2))(4x+7) & \\le 0 \\\\ \\frac{(-1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(|x|+2))(4x+7)}{(-1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(|x|+2))} & \\ge \\frac{0}{(-1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(|x|+2))} \\\\ 4x+7 & \\ge 0 \\\\ 4x & \\ge -7 \\\\ x & \\ge -\\frac{7}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534401 true] [17592186286393 1000 #edb/ref 17592186286394 13194139534401 true] [17592186286393 1000 #edb/ref 17592186286395 13194139534401 true] [17592186286393 1000 #edb/ref 17592186286396 13194139534401 true] [17592186286393 1000 #edb/ref 17592186286397 13194139534401 true] [17592186286393 1000 #edb/ref 17592186286398 13194139534401 true] [17592186286393 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534401 true] [17592186286393 1041 "selection" 13194139534401 true] [17592186286393 1104 #edb/ref 17592186286396 13194139534401 true] [17592186286393 1110 #uuid "82d05bf0-fece-5310-8126-59f79ab3416c" 13194139534401 true] [17592186286394 1034 "x\\ge \\frac{1}{2}" 13194139534401 true] [17592186286394 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534401 true] [17592186286394 1111 #uuid "359005b3-e88f-525d-b35b-69efb7236dd5" 13194139534401 true] [17592186286395 1034 "x\\le \\frac{7}{4}" 13194139534401 true] [17592186286395 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534401 true] [17592186286395 1111 #uuid "4c2ece7c-8b84-502e-9197-fa70c2476266" 13194139534401 true] [17592186286396 1034 "x\\ge -\\frac{7}{4}" 13194139534401 true] [17592186286396 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534401 true] [17592186286396 1111 #uuid "0e3c7d12-b56d-5aa4-86ae-d91948a186e1" 13194139534401 true] [17592186286397 1034 "x\\ge \\frac{3}{4}" 13194139534401 true] [17592186286397 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534401 true] [17592186286397 1111 #uuid "e940f551-0f01-5c56-bc7e-5f2e91a85df5" 13194139534401 true] [17592186286398 1034 "x\\le -\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139534401 true] [17592186286398 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534401 true] [17592186286398 1111 #uuid "b806af84-e00a-50b7-83be-af5a35cf98d6" 13194139534401 true] [17592186286399 1017 #edb/ref 17592186286400 13194139534401 true] [17592186286399 1089 "q-116265" 13194139534401 true] [17592186286399 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534401 true] [17592186286399 1100 "Solve the inequality\n\n$$\n(2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+3)-3\\pi )(2x+9)<0.\n$$" 13194139534401 true] [17592186286399 1113 #uuid "079d429f-ce2e-5460-8272-295a0ca5238b" 13194139534401 true] [17592186286399 1145 "Note that the factor $2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+3)-3\\pi $ is always negative. We know this because\n\n$$\n-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+3)<\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,⇒\\,-4\\pi <2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+3)-3\\pi <-2\\pi .\n$$\n\nSince $2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+3)-3\\pi $ is always negative, we can divide both sides of the inequality by this quantity, but we have to remember to flip the sign of the inequality. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+3)-3\\pi )(2x+9) & <0 \\\\ \\frac{(2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+3)-3\\pi )(2x+9)}{2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+3)-3\\pi } & >\\frac{0}{2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+3)-3\\pi } \\\\ 2x+9 & >0 \\\\ 2x & >-9 \\\\ x & >-\\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534401 true] [17592186286400 1000 #edb/ref 17592186286401 13194139534401 true] [17592186286400 1000 #edb/ref 17592186286402 13194139534401 true] [17592186286400 1000 #edb/ref 17592186286403 13194139534401 true] [17592186286400 1000 #edb/ref 17592186286404 13194139534401 true] [17592186286400 1000 #edb/ref 17592186286405 13194139534401 true] [17592186286400 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534401 true] [17592186286400 1041 "selection" 13194139534401 true] [17592186286400 1104 #edb/ref 17592186286405 13194139534401 true] [17592186286400 1110 #uuid "4d1b9d0e-b2bd-5c3b-8fbd-578a1323f298" 13194139534401 true] [17592186286401 1034 "x<-\\frac{3}{2}" 13194139534401 true] [17592186286401 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534401 true] [17592186286401 1111 #uuid "6037e0d5-3e9e-553a-a31a-fca34ace9bf4" 13194139534401 true] [17592186286402 1034 "x>\\frac{3}{2}" 13194139534401 true] [17592186286402 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534401 true] [1